9.2  The kernel command line

TL;DR
  - The kernel command line is the list of options the bootloader passes to the
    kernel: which disk is root, quiet or verbose boot, driver settings.
  - The current one is in /proc/cmdline.
  - For one boot, edit the entry in the bootloader menu (e in GRUB). To keep a
    change, edit the bootloader's settings.

      cat /proc/cmdline

On your machine
  Firmware  UEFI · Secure Boot on
  Kernel    6.12.48-amd64
  Cmdline   BOOT_IMAGE=/vmlinuz-6.12.48-amd64
            root=UUID=3f1c2a9e-8b7d-4e21-9c55-0a6b2d7e9f10 ro rootflags=subvol=@
            quiet splash
  Init      systemd · default target graphical.target
  Up        3 days, 4 h

Pitfalls
  - With GRUB, editing GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX in /etc/default/grub does nothing
    until you regenerate its config: sudo update-grub on Debian and Ubuntu,
    sudo grub-mkconfig -o /boot/grub/grub.cfg on Arch.
  - A typo in root= makes the system unbootable: try an option once from the
    menu before making it permanent.
  - quiet hides the boot messages: remove it to see where a boot hangs.
  - systemd.unit=rescue.target boots into a rescue shell, for one boot.

Deep dive

  ▾ Who reads these options?

    The kernel takes the ones it knows, such as root= or mitigations=. Options
    with a module name and a dot go to that module: i915.enable_psr=0 is for the
    Intel graphics driver. The kernel hands the rest to init, and systemd reads
    the ones that start with systemd..

  ▾ What do the common options do?

    ro mounts root read-only at first, so it can be checked; init remounts it
    read-write afterwards. quiet keeps the boot silent, and splash shows a boot
    animation instead. nomodeset keeps the kernel's graphics drivers off, a
    common workaround when a new graphics card shows a black screen.

Related
  Commands  cat
  Paths     /proc/cmdline, /etc/default/grub
  Hub       idk boot Boot
  See also  idk 9.1 From firmware to init
